nodecode-channel-chatwork is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-chatwork:

  https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-plugin-chatwork
  commit 8dbdff609b3c99768420a19a7ca646b6daad3dbe
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the lane as a whole -- polling Chatwork's REST API v2 for
the rooms an account is in and the newest messages of each, deciding which of
them were addressed to the bot (a [To:the bot] tag, or a reply to one of its
own messages, in a group room; anything in My Chat and in a direct chat;
free-response rooms and a mention switch for the rest), answering as
Chatwork's own Reply button does, with the [rp aid=.. to=room-message] line
that notifies the asker, reading Chatwork's notation in ([code], [qt],
[title], [info], [download:N], [url]) and writing it out ([code] for a fence,
【...】 for a heading, no **bold**, and notification tags defused so a
prompt-injected [toall] cannot ping a company room), the durable read
position that keeps a restart from answering a message twice, and the
rate-limit discipline that keeps a poll inside 300 calls per five minutes.
All of it was reimplemented in Common Lisp against Nodecode's channel cell
contract, so the rooms, lanes, gate, cards and delivery are the channel
kit's rather than the plugin's own gateway plumbing.

What was left out and why: the Hermes gateway adapter class and its config
plumbing (PlatformConfig, SendResult, MessageEvent, the token lease, the
plugin-data folder) are the harness's own and became a channels.chatwork
section and a state file under the Nodecode home. The plugin's own
guest-command rule -- while CHATWORK_ALLOWED_USERS is unset, only the
token's account may run Hermes commands or answer an approval -- is the
channel kit's operator rule now (declare owner), because the commands and
approvals it guards are Nodecode's, not Hermes's. reply_to_text quoting of
the replied-to message is not carried: the lane's session already holds the
conversation it is answering in. Private notices (notice_delivery: private)
and the /sethome delivery room are the host cell's delivery, not this
folder's; and a card is never deleted, only answered under, because Chatwork
cannot delete a message.
